{0 Query links}

A query link names a note by its frontmatter instead of its path:
[[[?id==foo]]] links to the one note whose frontmatter has [id: foo]. Once it
resolves, it behaves like a wikilink to that note's name: a fragment, an
alias and the embed form all work as usual, as in
[![[?id==foo#Intro|the intro]]].

Implemented by {!Oystermark.Note.Query} and
{!Oystermark.Vault.Index.resolve}.

{1:syntax Syntax}

A wikilink whose target, after blanks are stripped, starts with [?] is a query
link. The rest of the target, up to the fragment's [#], is an
{{:https://github.com/jirutka/rsql-parser}RSQL} expression.

{v
expression = or
or         = and { "," and }
and        = constraint { ";" constraint }
constraint = "(" or ")" | selector operator arguments
arguments  = "(" value { "," value } ")" | value
value      = unreserved | '"' chars '"' | "'" chars "'"
v}

{ul
  {- A {e selector} is a top-level frontmatter key, written as unreserved
     characters: anything but whitespace and [" ' ( ) ; , = ! ~ < >].}
  {- The operators are [==], [!=], [=lt=] or [<], [=le=] or [<=], [=gt=] or
     [>], [=ge=] or [>=], [=in=] and [=out=]. Only [=in=] and [=out=] take a
     parenthesized list of several values.}
  {- In a quoted value, a backslash escapes the next character.}
  {- Blanks between tokens are ignored.}
  {- [;] binds tighter than [,]: [a==1;b==2,c==3] is [(a==1;b==2),c==3].}
}

[#] and [|] cannot appear anywhere in the query, even inside quotes: a wikilink
is split at its first [#] into target and fragment, and at its [|] into target
and alias, before the query is read.

Only wikilinks are query links. A Markdown link [[text](?id=foo)] keeps its URL
meaning, a query on the current page, and is not a vault link.

The keyword spellings [and] and [or], and wildcards in values, are not
supported.

{1:matching Matching}

A note's frontmatter is matched against the query by these rules:

{ul
  {- A selector names a top-level key. A key that is absent, or frontmatter
     that is not a mapping, gives no values.}
  {- A sequence gives its items as values; any other value gives itself. A
     comparison holds when it holds for {e some} value, so [tags==draft]
     matches [tags: [draft, essay]].}
  {- A value is compared as a YAML scalar. A number is compared numerically
     when the argument reads as a number, so [year==2026] and [year==2026.0]
     both match [year: 2026]. Otherwise the scalar's text is compared with the
     argument as strings: [draft==false] matches both [draft: false] and
     [draft: "false"], and [created=lt=2026-01-01] compares [YYYY-MM-DD] dates
     in order.}
  {- A null, a mapping, and a sequence nested in a sequence never match.}
  {- [==] holds when some value equals the argument, [=in=] when some value
     equals some argument. [!=] and [=out=] are their negations, so
     [draft!=true] matches a note without [draft].}
  {- The ordering operators hold when some value is ordered against the
     argument that way.}
}

{1:resolution Resolution}

The query is evaluated against every note in the vault, including the one
that contains the link. Assets have no frontmatter and never match.

{ul
  {- Exactly one match: the link resolves to that note, and then its fragment,
     if any, is resolved in that note as for any wikilink.}
  {- A query that does not parse, no match, or several matches: the link does
     not resolve, with the reason
     {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.Invalid_query},
     {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.No_match} or
     {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.Ambiguous}.}
}

An ordinary link that does not resolve may name a note yet to be written. A
query link that does not resolve cannot, so clients treat it as an error:

{ul
  {- [oyster check] reports it as an error and exits non-zero.}
  {- The language server reports it with its reason, see
     {!page-"feature-diagnostics".resolution_check}.}
  {- The publisher fails the build, listing every such link in a published
     note. It parses frontmatter itself with YAML's core schema, under which a
     date is the string it was written as, as it is for the other clients.}
}

A query depends on every note's frontmatter, so editing one note can change
where a link in another resolves. Every resolution is computed from the
current vault snapshot.

Moving or renaming a note never rewrites a query link: the query does not
mention the note's path. A heading rename still rewrites the fragment of a
query link that points at that heading.

{1:display Display}

The publisher displays a query link without an alias as the resolved note's
name, not the query. An unresolved one displays the query as written.
